"""Same-call, subject-independent source relation checks."""
VERSION="source-relation-grounding-v2"
RULES="\nBefore asserting a signed difference, ratio direction, residual, or an error-table\nconvention, check one supplied pair of operands against its PRINTED signed value.\nName the subtraction/division order that matches those values; do not replace the\nsource convention with a familiar one. Preserve the printed data when paraphrasing.\nDistinguish the graph's edges from its displayed execution frames: a possible next\nalgorithm step is not a state shown in the source. Describe that distinction only\nwhere needed to explain the lesson; never silently add a state to the shown trace.\nKeep a symbol's meaning consistent within a derivation; if a coefficient already\nuses C, use another symbol for a newly introduced arbitrary constant. Keep internal\nblock letters, verification notes and implementation bookkeeping out of learner prose.\n\nFor a retained threshold or inequality, test a value on each side of the boundary\nagainst its equation before explaining which quantity is insufficient or excessive.\nUse that SAME inequality direction in easy explanations. For a vector component,\nname the positive direction or explicitly explain a magnitude; do not confuse a\npositive magnitude with a signed component. Keep left/right/central descriptions\ninside the depicted figure's own frame and match named positions to source labels.\n"
